<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Gnu on cesun.life</title><link>https://example.org/tags/gnu/</link><description>Recent content in Gnu on cesun.life</description><generator>Hugo</generator><language>en</language><lastBuildDate>Tue, 19 May 2026 10:53:04 +0800</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://example.org/tags/gnu/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>GNU Bison</title><link>https://example.org/gnu-bison/</link><pubDate>Tue, 19 May 2026 10:53:04 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://example.org/gnu-bison/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;GNU Bison generates parsers from context-free grammars. Its Yacc-compatible notation defines tokens, nonterminals, semantic values, and actions; the generated parser obtains tokens from a scanner by convention. Consult the &lt;a href="https://www.gnu.org/software/bison/manual/"&gt;GNU Bison manual&lt;/a&gt; for skeleton- and language-specific interfaces.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id="grammar-sections-values-and-actions"&gt;
 Grammar Sections, Values, and Actions
 &lt;a href="#grammar-sections-values-and-actions" class="heading-anchor"&gt;
 &lt;span&gt;#&lt;/span&gt;
 &lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;A grammar has declarations, productions, and optional epilogue code, separated by &lt;code&gt;%%&lt;/code&gt;. Declarations define tokens, value types, precedence, parser settings, and required code. Productions define nonterminals and actions.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>awk</title><link>https://example.org/awk/</link><pubDate>Wed, 22 May 2024 06:31:54 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://example.org/awk/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;awk&lt;/code&gt; transforms text as a stream of records. Each record is tested against
ordered pattern-action rules, which makes the language suited to reports,
validation, and small data transformations. POSIX &lt;code&gt;awk&lt;/code&gt; is widely available;
GNU awk (&lt;code&gt;gawk&lt;/code&gt;) adds features that scripts should declare when they require
them.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id="records-fields-and-counters"&gt;
 Records, Fields, and Counters
 &lt;a href="#records-fields-and-counters" class="heading-anchor"&gt;
 &lt;span&gt;#&lt;/span&gt;
 &lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;RS&lt;/code&gt; separates input records and defaults to newline. &lt;code&gt;FS&lt;/code&gt; separates fields;
its default treats runs of spaces and tabs specially. &lt;code&gt;$0&lt;/code&gt; is the whole record,
&lt;code&gt;$1&lt;/code&gt; through &lt;code&gt;$NF&lt;/code&gt; are fields, and &lt;code&gt;NF&lt;/code&gt; is the field count. &lt;code&gt;NR&lt;/code&gt; counts records
across all inputs, whereas &lt;code&gt;FNR&lt;/code&gt; restarts for each input file.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>GNU Autotools</title><link>https://example.org/gnu-autotools/</link><pubDate>Tue, 14 May 2024 21:47:22 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://example.org/gnu-autotools/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;GNU Autotools generates portable build interfaces from project metadata. Autoconf probes the build environment and emits &lt;code&gt;configure&lt;/code&gt;; Automake generates portable &lt;code&gt;Makefile.in&lt;/code&gt; templates from &lt;code&gt;Makefile.am&lt;/code&gt;; Libtool abstracts platform-specific library linking. The generated files are designed for source releases, whereas maintainers edit the &lt;code&gt;.ac&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;.am&lt;/code&gt;, and m4 inputs.&lt;/p&gt;
