 &lt;/div&gt;&lt;h2 id="input-and-generated-files"&gt;
 Input and Generated Files
 &lt;a href="#input-and-generated-files" class="heading-anchor"&gt;
 &lt;span&gt;#&lt;/span&gt;
 &lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;A project normally contains these inputs:&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Flex</title><link>https://example.org/flex/</link><pubDate>Tue, 10 Jan 2023 06:39:13 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://example.org/flex/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;Flex generates a scanner from rules that map input text to C or C++ actions. A scanner normally supplies tokens to a parser, but it can also transform text or validate a regular-language format. The &lt;a href="https://westes.github.io/flex/manual/"&gt;Flex manual&lt;/a&gt; defines generated interfaces and option-specific behavior.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id="specification-structure"&gt;
 Specification Structure
 &lt;a href="#specification-structure" class="heading-anchor"&gt;
 &lt;span&gt;#&lt;/span&gt;
 &lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;A &lt;code&gt;.l&lt;/code&gt; file has three sections separated by &lt;code&gt;%%&lt;/code&gt;: definitions, rules, and user code. Definitions hold C declarations, named regular expressions, and start conditions. Rules pair patterns with actions. The final section supplies helper functions and, when needed, a driver.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>GNU Info</title><link>https://example.org/gnu-info/</link><pubDate>Thu, 23 Dec 2021 11:03:52 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://example.org/gnu-info/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;GNU Info is the terminal reader for manuals generated from Texinfo. It is not a
pager for a linear text file: an Info &lt;em&gt;file&lt;/em&gt; contains named nodes joined by
menus, cross-references, and &lt;code&gt;Next&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;Prev&lt;/code&gt;, and &lt;code&gt;Up&lt;/code&gt; pointers. An installed
manual can be split across several compressed filesystem files, but Info
presents it as one manual.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id="start-with-the-manual-tree"&gt;
 Start with the Manual Tree
 &lt;a href="#start-with-the-manual-tree" class="heading-anchor"&gt;
 &lt;span&gt;#&lt;/span&gt;
 &lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;Run &lt;code&gt;info&lt;/code&gt; without an argument to open the directory node, the installed
manual tree. Run &lt;code&gt;info coreutils&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;info '(coreutils)Top'&lt;/code&gt; to open a named
manual and node.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item></channel></rss>